-              <p>Under Assumptions F1 to F4, if \(\lambda_j = 0\), then \(Aq_j = 0\) with \(q_j \ne 0\), which contradicts the invertibility of \(A\) by F1. So every \(\lambda_j \ne 0\), and \(\Lambda^{-1}\) exists. Then \[(Q\Lambda^{-1}Q^{\top})(Q\Lambda Q^{\top}) = Q\Lambda^{-1}\Lambda Q^{\top} = QQ^{\top} = I\], using Lemma 1.4 for \(QQ^{\top} = I\). By F1 the product \(Q\Lambda^{-1}Q^{\top}\) is \(A^{-1}\).</p>
+              <p>Under Assumptions F1 to F4, if \(\lambda_j = 0\), then \(Aq_j = 0\) with \(q_j \ne 0\), which contradicts the invertibility of \(A\) by F1. So every \(\lambda_j \ne 0\), and \(\Lambda^{-1}\) exists. Then \[(Q\Lambda^{-1}Q^{\top})(Q\Lambda Q^{\top}) = Q\Lambda^{-1}\Lambda Q^{\top} = QQ^{\top} = I\], where the first equality uses \(Q^{\top}Q = I\) (Definition 1.2), the second uses \(\Lambda^{-1}\Lambda = I\), which holds because every \(\lambda_j \ne 0\), and the last uses \(QQ^{\top} = I\) (Lemma 1.4). By F1 the product \(Q\Lambda^{-1}Q^{\top}\) is \(A^{-1}\).</p>
