             <p>Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, let \(A\) be \(m \times n\) with \(m < n\). Prove that \(A^{\top}A\) has at least \(n - m\) zero eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>The system \(Ax = 0\) has \(n\) unknowns and \(m\) equations, so the rank of \(A\) is at most \(m\). By rank–nullity (Assumption F4 of Lesson 4) the null space \(N = \{x : Ax = 0\}\) has dimension \(n - \operatorname{rank} A \ge n - m\). Let \(E_0 = \{x : A^{\top}Ax = 0\}\). If \(x \in E_0\), then \(\|Ax\|^2 = x^{\top}A^{\top}Ax = 0\), so \(Ax = 0\). Conversely \(Ax = 0\) gives \(A^{\top}Ax = 0\). So \(E_0 = N\), and \(\dim E_0 \ge n - m\).</p>
+              <p>The system \(Ax = 0\) has \(n\) unknowns and \(m\) equations, so the rank of \(A\) is at most \(m\). By rank–nullity (Assumption F4 of Lesson 4) the null space \(K = \{x : Ax = 0\}\) has dimension \(n - \operatorname{rank} A \ge n - m\). Let \(E_0 = \{x : A^{\top}Ax = 0\}\). If \(x \in E_0\), then \(\|Ax\|^2 = x^{\top}A^{\top}Ax = 0\), so \(Ax = 0\). Conversely \(Ax = 0\) gives \(A^{\top}Ax = 0\). So \(E_0 = K\), and \(\dim E_0 \ge n - m\).</p>
               <p>Now count with multiplicity. Under Assumptions F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, Theorem 2.3 of Lesson 4 gives an orthonormal basis \(v_1, \ldots, v_n\) of \(\R^n\) with \(A^{\top}Av_j = \lambda_j v_j\). The characteristic polynomial of \(A^{\top}A\) is \(\prod_j (x - \lambda_j)\), so the multiplicity of \(0\) as an eigenvalue is \(\#\{j : \lambda_j = 0\}\). Let \(x = \sum_j c_j v_j\) be in \(E_0\). Then \(0 = A^{\top}Ax = \sum_j c_j \lambda_j v_j\), so \(c_j \lambda_j = 0\) for every \(j\), and \(c_j = 0\) whenever \(\lambda_j \ne 0\). Hence \(E_0\) is spanned by the \(v_j\) with \(\lambda_j = 0\), which lie in \(E_0\) and are independent. So \(\dim E_0 = \#\{j : \lambda_j = 0\}\), and the multiplicity of \(0\) equals \(\dim E_0 \ge n - m\).</p>
